---
interface Props {
  id?: string;
}
const { id } = Astro.props;

// The same blink, built three ways for an Arduino Uno. TOTAL flash (interrupt
// vector table + startup stub included), so the numbers are apples-to-apples:
// every variant toggles PB5 and waits 500 ms. This is not a "PyMCU vs. C"
// benchmark — PyMCU isn't trying to out-compile C. The hand-written C number
// is shown only as a neutral size floor, not a target. PyMCU and C are
// measured with the same toolchain PyMCU ships (avr-gcc 7.3.0, -Os,
// F_CPU=16 MHz); Arduino is the IDE's own AVR core (avr-gcc
// 7.3.0-atmel3.6.1) via arduino-cli.
// Measured 2026-09-15 with pymcu-compiler 0.1.0a10 and arduino-cli 1.5.1 /
// Arduino AVR core 1.8.8.
const max = 1058;
const bars = [
  { name: 'PyMCU', sub: 'native HAL / MicroPython', bytes: 142, label: '142 B', win: true },
  { name: 'C', sub: 'avr-gcc -Os, size reference', bytes: 162, label: '162 B', win: false },
  { name: 'Arduino', sub: 'IDE defaults', bytes: 1058, label: '1,058 B', win: false },
];
---

<section {id} class="py-12 sm:py-16 lg:py-20 mx-auto max-w-5xl px-6">
  <div class="mb-8 text-center">
    <p class="text-base font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-accent mb-2">Native firmware</p>
    <h2 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 dark:text-white">
      Python, sized for the chips that never had room for it
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 max-w-2xl mx-auto text-sm">
      The same blink — toggle the LED, wait 500&nbsp;ms — total flash with the vector table and startup included.
      Hand-written C is on the chart only as a size reference: PyMCU isn't trying to out-compile C, it's trying to
      bring Python to the boards an interpreter can't fit on.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- astral.sh-style benchmark card -->
  <div class="rounded-2xl bg-gray-900 dark:bg-gray-950 ring-1 ring-white/10 shadow-xl p-6 sm:p-10">
    <p class="mb-8 text-xs sm:text-sm text-gray-400 tracking-wide">
      Blink firmware for an Arduino Uno — smaller is better.
    </p>

    <ul class="space-y-7">
      {
        bars.map((b) => (
          <li>
            <div class="relative h-2.5 w-full rounded-full bg-white/10 overflow-hidden">
              <div
                class:list={['absolute inset-y-0 left-0 rounded-full', b.win ? 'bg-accent' : 'bg-gray-500']}
                style={`width:${Math.max((b.bytes / max) * 100, 4)}%`}
              />
            </div>
            <div class="mt-2.5 flex items-baseline justify-between text-xs tracking-widest">
              <span class="flex items-baseline gap-2">
                <span class:list={['font-semibold uppercase', b.win ? 'text-accent' : 'text-gray-200']}>{b.name}</span>
                <span class="normal-case tracking-normal text-[11px] text-gray-500">{b.sub}</span>
              </span>
              <span class:list={['font-mono tabular-nums', b.win ? 'text-accent' : 'text-gray-400']}>{b.label}</span>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>

    <p class="mt-9 border-t border-white/10 pt-6 text-xs text-gray-500">
      Why 142 bytes? 102 of them are the interrupt-vector table every AVR program carries, whatever wrote it. What's
      actually yours is the remaining <span class="font-semibold text-gray-300">40 bytes</span>: the startup stub and
      the blink loop. Arduino's extra bytes aren't waste either; they're the core's conveniences (<code
        >millis()</code
      >, preconfigured timers, one sketch for every board). PyMCU includes only what your program uses, which is why
      a whole program can fit in a few hundred bytes: on a chip with 32&nbsp;KB of flash total, that is what leaves
      room for the parts that matter, like a sensor driver or a protocol handler, without running out of space.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- Friendly framing: Python on MCUs is mainstream, Arduino included -->
  <div
    class="mt-6 rounded-2xl border border-accent/30 bg-accent/5 dark:bg-accent/10 p-5 text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300"
  >
    Python on microcontrollers is going mainstream — Arduino itself now offers official
    <span class="font-semibold">MicroPython</span> support. We think that direction is exactly right. PyMCU shares the goal
    of Python-friendly embedded and takes it all the way to the bare metal: the same familiar syntax, compiled to native
    machine code with no interpreter on the chip. On September 15, 2026, Adafruit's own unmodified
    <code>adafruit_hcsr04</code> library, running its own example verbatim, compiled and read distances on an Arduino
    Uno in 3,432&nbsp;bytes of flash (measured against compiler commit <code>2bc83f4b</code>; the number has already
    dropped twice today as bugs closed, and will keep moving). It's the first library to make that jump unedited, not
    a claim that every library does yet.
  </div>
</section>
